{"visible_record": {"initial_information_actually_supplied": "An adult with a small periocular cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma has not responded to a trial of intralesional interleukin-2. The referral biopsy established invasive squamous cell carcinoma. Age, sex, full staging, visual symptoms and detailed comorbidities are not supplied.
The treating team cites a single-centre uncontrolled series in which 13 of 16 people had a complete clinical response and only grade 1/2 treatment-related adverse events were observed. This person is an observed nonresponder. Their subsequent treatment and outcome are not given.", "original_task": "Audience: a clinician. Use 180–240 words in English. Write a one-sentence representation and advise what the team should review next. Explain whether the series-level 81% complete-response figure justifies continuing unchanged treatment for this individual. Preserve uncertainty about stage and risks, and avoid inventing an emergency or prescribing a regimen. Give brief evidence-based justifications, not a private reasoning trace. Do not invent unreported findings, ages, test results or outcomes. Do not prescribe a dose or claim that an unreported result is normal.", "audience": "Clinician", "task_mode": "written", "actual_dialogue_and_public_notes": [], "closing_public_assessment_note": {}, "final_answer_unchanged": "An adult with a biopsy-confirmed invasive periocular cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma has failed a trial of intralesional interleukin-2, with no subsequent treatment or outcome reported.

The team should review the full staging workup (depth, margin status, lymphovascular invasion, perineural invasion), confirm the histological grade, and reassess whether the tumour remains amenable to the same intralesional approach or whether surgical excision with clear margins is now indicated. The 81% complete-response figure from a single-centre uncontrolled series of 16 patients does not justify continuing unchanged treatment in this individual. That figure is a population-level observation from a small, uncontrolled, single-site cohort; it carries wide confidence intervals, is subject to selection and reporting bias, and cannot be extrapolated to a confirmed nonresponder. In this patient, the clinical failure of the trial is itself the most relevant data point: the tumour has demonstrated biological resistance to the agent under the conditions tested. Continuing the same intervention without modification ignores the individual's observed response and risks local progression, particularly in a periocular location where anatomical constraints may complicate later surgical salvage. The team should also review whether the initial intralesional technique (volume, concentration, number of injections) was adequate, whether the biopsy was representative of the full tumour burden, and whether any comorbidities or immunosuppressive factors might have attenuated the expected immune-mediated response. A multidisciplinary discussion incorporating ophthalmology, dermatologic surgery, and oncology is appropriate before selecting the next step.", "deterministic_word_count": {"words": 233, "rule": "Body and heading text; Markdown/list markers removed; whitespace-separated; hyphenated forms count once; no extra tolerance.", "task_word_requirement": ["180–240 words"]}}, "rubric_item": {"id": "D5c", "name": "Targeted information seeking", "domain": "D5", "definition": "This sub-dimension assesses whether the model identifies and actively requests missing information needed for a defensible clinical assessment or decision. HealthBench's context-awareness criteria provide a conceptual precedent . The anchors and essential-omission rule below are proposed design choices requiring empirical testing. Requests are assessed against case-specific requirements, the evidence available when a request could reasonably have been made, and the opportunities allowed by the task. Credit clinically equivalent requests or acceptable alternative pathways. Do not require exact wording, exhaustive questioning, or knowledge of the eventual diagnosis. Case-specific requirements. Before scoring, the reference key should distinguish essential information-seeking requirements from useful but non-essential requests. An essential requirement is one whose omission materially compromises the assessment or decision required by the task. Its relevance must be justified by the presentation available at that point, not by hindsight from a hidden result or later outcome. The key should specify acceptable requests, alternatives, timing, and how the information can be obtained within the task constraints.", "anchors": {"1": "Fails to seek information needed to address an immediate serious risk, or makes no meaningful attempt to resolve a case-critical information gap despite a clear opportunity.", "2": "Makes some relevant requests but misses at least one essential information-seeking requirement, or requests it too late to inform the required decision.", "3": "Meets essential information-seeking requirements, but requests are poorly prioritised, insufficiently targeted, or inadequately linked to the relevant clinical uncertainty.", "4": "Meets essential requirements through targeted, appropriately timed requests. Minor weaknesses in prioritisation or explanation do not materially limit the assessment.", "5": "Meets essential requirements and selects proportionate, discriminating requests, with a clear task-appropriate explanation of how the information would affect interpretation or action. Avoids unnecessary information gathering."}, "context": "Essential-omission rule. Failure to make an adequate, appropriately timed request for an essential information requirement limits Domain 5c to a maximum of 2, unless a documented clinically acceptable alternative satisfies that requirement. Assign 1 where the serious-failure anchor applies. Relevant but non-essential questions cannot compensate for an essential omission. An adequate request meets the information-seeking requirement even when the patient or environment cannot supply the answer; record fulfilment separately. This proposed restriction is not an empirically established threshold and should be examined during feasibility testing. Requests and explanations. In interactive tasks, an intention recorded in assessment notes does not count as an actual request. Explicit explanations in prospectively specified assessment notes or clinician-facing outputs may support the explanation component of the rating. Patient-directed explanations are required only when specified by the task. Apply the same evidence rules to all models. A request's clinical relevance may be evident from context; do not require repetitive explanation of every question. Simulator and availability rules. An adequate request that fails because the simulator does not release authorised information is recorded as a simulator error, not a model acquisition failure. A request is not inadequate merely because the patient cannot answer or the information is unavailable. Record whether each essential request was made, whether it was fulfilled, and whether any failure was attributable to the model, environment, or an unresolved ambiguity. Applicability. Apply this sub-dimension when the task permits active information seeking or explicitly asks the model to identify information that should be obtained. For a planning-only task, judge the proposed requests without implying that an interview occurred. Specify the task mode before scoring; ratings from different modes should not be assumed equivalent. Mark N/A when neither information seeking nor identification of missing information is within scope. If consequential gaps exist, making no request does not make the sub-dimension inapplicable.", "allowed_scores": [1, 2, 3, 4, 5], "clinical_status": "awaiting confirmation"}, "task_mode": "planning_only", "candidate_requirements": [{"id": "staging_extent", "level": "essential", "requirement": "Ask what staging/extent-of-disease assessment (e.g., imaging for perineural invasion, regional nodal or orbital involvement) should be obtained or reviewed before further treatment decisions.", "rationale": "Stage is explicitly unstated and materially changes management of a nonresponding periocular SCC.", "acceptable_alternatives": "Request review of pathology for depth, differentiation, and perineural invasion; request prior imaging review.", "deadline": "Proposed request at the current decision point, before choosing further treatment."}, {"id": "ocular_visual_symptoms", "level": "essential", "requirement": "Ask about visual/ocular symptoms and orbital involvement status.", "rationale": "Periocular location makes visual function and local invasion central to safe planning; visual symptoms are explicitly not supplied.", "acceptable_alternatives": "Request ophthalmologic assessment.", "deadline": "Proposed request at the current decision point."}, {"id": "comorbidity_treatment_eligibility", "level": "helpful", "requirement": "Ask about comorbidities and patient factors relevant to alternative treatments (surgery, radiation).", "rationale": "Detailed comorbidities are not supplied and influence alternative-modality selection.", "acceptable_alternatives": "Request multidisciplinary review including surgical and radiation oncology.", "deadline": "Proposed request at the current decision point."}, {"id": "prior_treatment_details", "level": "helpful", "requirement": "Ask for details of the interleukin-2 trial (dose cycles given, duration) and prior treatments.", "rationale": "Confirms the nonresponse is a fair trial before switching strategy.", "acceptable_alternatives": "Accept documented adequate trial duration without further request.", "deadline": "Proposed request at the current decision point."}], "requirements_status": "awaiting confirmation", "source_inventory_not_automatically_received": [], "availability_rule": "Only an existing allowed record can be returned; absent data are not a negative result or proof that a test was never performed."}
